{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he fell upon the necks of Benjamin his brother.</b> Scripture tells that Joseph fell upon the necks of Benjamin, and Benjamin also fell upon his neck, after the custom of brothers, sons of one mother, whose love is fierce, in the manner of what is written (Song of Songs 8:1): ״מי יתנך כאח לי יונק שדי אמי״, and as it is written (Deuteronomy 13:7): ״כי יסיתך אחיך בן אמך״. And as for why it mentions 'necks' in the plural — it is known that the neck, which is the back of a person, is called עורף, being opposite the face, in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 2:27): ״ערף ולא פנים״; and that which is in front of a person, as well as the sides, are also called צואר. Therefore Scripture mentioned 'necks' in the plural." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And he fell upon the necks of Benjamin his brother' — Joseph wept over the two Temples that were destined to be in the portion of Benjamin and destined to be destroyed. And because the neck is at the height of a person's body, he likened to it the Temple, which is on the height of the mountain. And Benjamin wept over the Tabernacle of Shiloh, which was destined to be in the portion of Joseph and destined to be destroyed."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:23.934358518Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}